-- A top-level value has no value of its own to read while it is being made,
-- so outside of a function its body does not see its name: `limit` here is
-- the one `use`d, as a `let` would have it. Inside a function it does, because
-- by the time the function runs the value is there.

use Limits (limit)

@pub def limit = limit + 1

@pub def countdown = \n -> if n == 0 then 0 else countdown (n - 1)

@pub def inPair = (\n -> if n == 0 then 0 else fst inPair (n - 1), let inPair = 2 in inPair)
